    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a> mining at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faynan">Faynan</a> and in the Timna Valley, dated to 1300–800 BCE, and the scale of the mines at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khirbat-en-nahas">Khirbat en-Nahas</a> are read as evidence of a strong, centralised Edomite kingdom in the 10th century BCE.</li><li>Assyrian inscriptions name three kings of Edom, Kaus-malaka, Aya-ramu and Kaus-gabri, between about 745 and 680 BCE; the last unambiguous reference to Edom is an Assyrian inscription of 667 BCE.</li><li>After the fall of the kingdom in the 6th century BCE the Edomites settled in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, where they were known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idumaeans">Idumaeans</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a> was of Edomite origin.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qos">Qaus</a>, the national god of Edom invoked in Edomite letters and personal names, is never mentioned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, unlike the gods of other nearby nations; some scholars explain the silence by his close likeness to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

