    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Edom lay in the south of present-day Jordan, east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabah">Arabah</a>, with the territory of Moab to its north; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zered">Zered</a>, now Wadi al-Hasa, marked the boundary between the two. The name comes from the Semitic root 'dm, meaning "man" and "red". Egyptian sources mention the land in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>: the list of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seti-i">Seti I</a> from c. 1215 BCE, the chronicle of a campaign by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii">Ramesses III</a>, and a letter from a scribe at a border fortress in the Wadi Tumilat under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a> reporting "shasu-tribes of Edom" moving to watering holes in Egyptian territory. The Edomites may have been connected with the Shasu and Shutu, the nomadic raiders of those records.</p><p>The earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> settlements, possibly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mining camps, date to the 11th century BCE. Copper samples from the Timna Valley and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faynan">Faynan</a>, dated to 1300–800 BCE, led archaeologists under Ezra Ben-Yosef and Tom Levy to argue in 2019 that a kingdom already existed, against the view that the Arava held only a loose alliance of tribes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> makes the Edomites the descendants of Esau, who settled the land after displacing the Horites; it was also called the land of Seir, and Mount Seir may have been a cultic site.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a> was the basis of Edom's wealth. Mining and smelting in the Arava valley, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faynan">Faynan</a> and in the Timna Valley, are dated to 1300–800 BCE, and the researchers who studied them concluded that Shoshenk I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, who attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the 10th century BCE, encouraged the trade and production of copper there instead of destroying the region. Settlement intensified by the late 8th century BCE, and the main sites so far excavated date between the 8th and 6th centuries BCE.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> gives Edom seaports at Eilat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezion-geber">Ezion-Geber</a>, and places the "King's Highway" through its land. Genesis names the clans of Esau, among them Timnah, Teman and Kenaz, each with its families and locality, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> calls their leaders aluf, a word it uses only for the dukes of Edom and Moab. The Edomite language was most similar to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biblical-hebrew">Biblical Hebrew</a> and Moabite. The national god appears to have been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qos">Qaus</a>, invoked in blessing formulas in letters and found in personal names; like other Levantine Semites the Edomites seem also to have worshipped El, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> and 'Uzza. Edomite and Israelite religious practices evolved in parallel, and Victor Sasson has described an Edomite text that parallels the Book of Job.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Most of what is told of Edom's wars comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> asked to pass along the King's Highway, the king of Edom refused and prepared to resist, though he did not attack. The Edomites are next recorded as defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saul">King Saul</a> in the late 11th century BCE; forty years later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">King David</a> and his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joab">Joab</a> defeated them in the "Valley of Salt", probably near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a>. An Edomite prince named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Hadad</a> escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, returned after David's death and failed in a rebellion. Edom then remained a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Israel and, after the division of the kingdom, a dependency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>.</p><p>In the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoshaphat">Jehoshaphat</a> the inhabitants of Mount Seir are said to have invaded Judea with Ammon and Moab. Edom later revolted against Jehoram and elected a king of its own. Amaziah (796–769 BCE) defeated the Edomites and seized Selah, but by the biblical account the Israelites never subdued Edom completely. Egyptian inscriptions record that the "Aduma" at times extended their possessions to the borders of Egypt. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 587 or 586 BCE, the Edomites may have helped plunder the city and slaughter the Judaeans, and Edom may have betrayed a treaty with Judah; Edomite settlement in southern Judah and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negev">Negev</a> had already begun, partly by military means.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Assyrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> inscriptions call the land Údumi or Údumu and name three of its kings: Kaus-malaka in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-iii">Tiglath-pileser III</a> (c. 745 BCE), Aya-ramu in that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> (c. 705 BCE) and Kaus-gabri in that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> (c. 680 BCE). These are the kings known from sources outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>; the last unambiguous reference to Edom is an Assyrian inscription of 667 BCE.</p><p>Genesis 36 lists eight kings who "reigned in the land of Edom before any king reigned over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>", from Bela son of Beor, whose city was Dinhabah, to Hadar, whose city was Pau. If the account may be taken at face value, the kingship was, at least in early times, not hereditary and perhaps elective. The First Book of Chronicles mentions both a king and chieftains. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bozrah">Bozrah</a>, now Busaira in Jordan, as the ancient capital. For the periods of rule by Israel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> it describes governors or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">prefects</a> placed over the Edomites by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, a form of government that seems to have continued under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, and, in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoshaphat">Jehoshaphat</a> (c. 870–849 BCE), a king of Edom who was probably a deputy appointed by the king of Judah. Edom's revolt against Jehoram ended that arrangement with a king elected by the Edomites themselves.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> of the Arava was the ground on which archaeologists argued in 2019 that a kingdom could exist there in the 10th century BCE; Ben Yosef said the findings were consistent with the biblical story of an Edomite kingdom. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> Edom is the brother nation descended from Esau and also an enemy denounced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremiah">Jeremiah</a> and Obadiah, perhaps for its part in the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. The Psalms say "over Edom will I cast out my shoe", and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> barred the descendants of a marriage between an Israelite and an Edomite from the congregation until the fourth generation, a law from which some early conversion laws in halacha were derived.</p><p>The Edomites outlived their kingdom. Settled in the hills south of Judea, they appear under the Greek name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idumaeans">Idumaeans</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hyrcanus">John Hyrcanus</a> I conquered Marisa and Adoraim and made the Idumaeans adopt Jewish law; some scholars have questioned the account since the late 1980s, though most historians still hold that it happened largely as he tells it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> the Idumaean, progenitor of the Herodian dynasty, was of Idumean origin, and so was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a>. In some Jewish traditions stemming from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a>, the descendants of Esau are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Edom flourished until the 8th century BCE and, after a period of decline, was destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> in the 6th. Its people had already begun to move west: excavations show Edomite settlement in the southern parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negev">Negev</a> down to Timna before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> destroyed the kingdom of Judah in 587/86 BCE, by peaceful penetration and by military means, taking advantage of Judah's weakness. Many Edomites migrated peacefully into southern Judea, a movement that continued under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a>, whose conquest in the 6th century BCE ended Edom as a state. Nomadic tribes from the east pushed the Edomites further west; among them were the Nabataeans, who had established their own kingdom in what had been Edom by the first half of the 2nd century BCE.</p><p>In the Persian period the Edomites settled the southern hills of Judea down to north of Be'er Sheva, and the name Edom disappeared from the land east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabah">Arabah</a>. Their new country was called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idumaea">Idumaea</a>, by 365 BCE a truncated Persian province. Ostraca of the 4th century BCE show <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, Edomites, Judeans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> living there, and settlement patterns and religious practice point to continuity with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> Edom. After the Jewish–Roman wars, in which 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idumaeans">Idumaeans</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zealots">Zealots</a> at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 70 CE according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, the Idumaean people disappear from written history.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Edom was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> kingdom east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabah">Arabah</a> whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mines at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faynan">Faynan</a> and Timna supported a centralised state by the 10th century BCE. Known from Egyptian, Assyrian and biblical sources, it fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in the 6th century BCE, and its people lived on in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idumaeans">Idumaeans</a>.</em></p>

